From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 20:01:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] drm/asahi: g16: Start the parameter buffer small and
 grow it on the overflow count

Every lazily backed parameter-buffer heap starts at the g16_pb_lazy floor
of 64 blocks (8 MiB). Hyprland and quickshell each hold two full units,
so an idle desktop keeps 32 MiB of parameter buffer. macOS idles at
8.25 MiB for all of its clients together and grows from there under load.

The floor exists because nothing else could grow the heap: the in-flight
free-block ring words are a save image that the firmware rewrites at the
fragment post, so they always read the seed and the 3x growth rule never
fires. A render that needs more than its manager holds is finished by the
hardware in several passes; it does not fault.

The fragment start record points the firmware at a word in the render's
pass scene (+0x54), in the slot where the G14X start record points at the
render's TVB overflow count. With g16_pb_grow on, that word is read when
the render retires. A non-zero value marks the unit's slot, and the VM's
next submit asks for twice the unit's current backing through the
existing growth step, capped at the heap window. Leased managers prepared
below the new backing are not reused, so the new blocks reach the next
render. With the overflow count to grow on, a lazily backed heap now
starts at 16 blocks (2 MiB) when its unit is built, and a full unit
reaches its 2048-block window in seven doublings, within the eight
growth steps.

g16_pb_grow defaults to 1. 0 reads nothing and keeps the g16_pb_lazy
floor, as before. A manager may now be seeded with as few as 16 blocks,
and g16_pb_size no longer asks for more blocks than the heap holds.

